  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "mogSsyt6udBu1wNphbze4Is5Lbs=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:43.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Build a reactor with nothing but document models registered and you get an **in-memory** PGlite database. The builder calls an internal `createDefaultDatabase()` that constructs `new Kysely({ dialect: new PGliteDialect(new PGlite()) })` \u2014 and `new PGlite()` with no arguments is in-memory. Nothing is written to disk and nothing survives the process.\n\n```typescript\nconst reactor = await new ReactorBuilder()\n  .withDocumentModelSources([myDocumentModel])\n  .build();\n```\n\n`buildModule()` picks the base database in a strict precedence order:\n\n1. The instance passed to `withKysely(...)`, if any.\n2. A Postgres pool, if the worker pool is enabled and a worker `db` config is set.\n3. The in-memory PGlite default.\n\nSo persistence is never inferred \u2014 there is no auto-detection, no environment sniffing. If you did not pass a Kysely instance or a worker-pool `db`, your data is gone at process exit. That is the correct backend for unit tests and local development (fresh isolated database every run, no external service), and a silent data-loss bug in anything else.\n\nWhat you get on the default path: migrations run automatically (strategy defaults to `\"auto\"`, running `runMigrations(db, REACTOR_SCHEMA)`), all storage under the `\"reactor\"` Postgres schema, a single in-process executor (`maxConcurrency` defaults to `1`), an in-process read-model coordinator, and `module.pools === []` since there is no `pg.Pool` to instrument.\n\nMove to Postgres when state must survive a restart, when more than one process must share storage, or when you scale \u2014 and note that **neither scaling path can run on PGlite at all**, because PGlite cannot be shared across threads. Both `withWorkerPool` and `withProjectionShards` therefore require a real Postgres server.\n\nFor `\"manual\"` or `\"none\"` migration strategies, run migrations yourself. `runMigrations` never throws on a migration error \u2014 it returns `{ success, migrationsExecuted, error? }`, so you must check the result:\n\n```typescript\nconst result = await runMigrations(db, REACTOR_SCHEMA);\nif (!result.success && result.error) throw result.error;\n```\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/07-StorageAndScaling.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:43.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:43.000Z"
    }
  },
